# Wick — Controls

This file defines how a player operates the game: the eight actions, the keys
bound to each, what each screen reads of them, and what the pointer does. The
movement vector and `facing` the held directions drive are in `specs/world.md`,
the weapons that aim by facing are in `specs/weapons.md`, and what each screen
shows is in `specs/ui.md`. Every figure below carries the name this
specification gives it. Every menu also answers the mouse.

## Where input comes from

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
This game stands on no engine, so the keyboard belongs to the runtime layer you
write. That layer maps each key in `BINDINGS` to its action in `ACTIONS` by
`KeyboardEvent.code`, so a binding is a physical key whatever the keyboard
layout, and it reports each action two ways: as a held value, `1` while any of
its keys is down and `0` otherwise, and as a press edge, true once for the frame
in which the held value went from `0` to `1`. A key event whose `repeat` flag is
set arms no edge. `LAYOUT` (`dpad-4`) names the control scheme: a four-way pad
whose vocabulary is the four movement actions, with the menu actions beside
them. The diagnostics overlay toggles on `Backquote`, outside the action
registry.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The {{engine.name}} engine delivers the input, and its documentation defines
the API. `src/main.ts` builds the engine with `LAYOUT` (`dpad-4`), so the four
movement actions are the layout's own vocabulary and the menu actions come with
it, and `initialize` registers every name in `ACTIONS` against its keys in
`BINDINGS`. `update` reads a held value with `value` and a press edge with
`pressed`, once per frame. The diagnostics overlay's toggle, the backtick, is
the engine's own.
{{else}}
The {{engine.name}} engine delivers the input, and its documentation defines
the API. `src/main.ts` builds the engine with `LAYOUT` (`dpad-4`), so the four
movement actions are the layout's own vocabulary and the menu actions come with
it, and the instance's `initialize` registers every name in `ACTIONS` against
its keys in `BINDINGS`. The player controller the game mode adds is the one
place they are read: a held value with `value` and a press edge with `pressed`,
once per frame. The diagnostics overlay's toggle, the backtick, is the engine's
own.
{{/if}}

## Actions and bindings

`ACTIONS` holds the eight actions the game registers, and `BINDINGS` maps each
to the `KeyboardEvent.code` values that fire it.

| Action | Binding | Read as | Does |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `up` | `ArrowUp`, `KeyW` | held on `playing`, edge elsewhere | moves the lamplighter up; moves a menu highlight up |
| `down` | `ArrowDown`, `KeyS` | held on `playing`, edge elsewhere | moves the lamplighter down; moves a menu highlight down |
| `left` | `ArrowLeft`, `KeyA` | held on `playing`, edge on `almanac` | moves the lamplighter left; moves the almanac's tab left |
| `right` | `ArrowRight`, `KeyD` | held on `playing`, edge on `almanac` | moves the lamplighter right; moves the almanac's tab right |
| `confirm` | `Enter`, `Space` | edge | accepts the highlighted item; closes the chest overlay |
| `back` | `Escape` | edge | leaves the screen, as the screen table states |
| `pause` | `KeyP` | edge | pauses on `playing`; resumes on `paused` |
| `mute` | `KeyM` | edge | toggles sound, on every screen |

The two keys bound to an action are interchangeable: `KeyW` does exactly what
`ArrowUp` does wherever `up` is read. All of a frame's edges are read against
the screen the frame began on, so a press that changes screens never also acts
on the screen it lands in. The frame's update then runs on the screen the
edges left: a frame whose press enters `playing`, from the title, an end
screen, `paused`, or an overlay, runs that frame's ticks, so a frame of
`TICK_DT` whose press lights the lamp leaves the run at tick `1`; a frame
whose press leaves `playing` ticks nothing and discards the accumulator.

## Moving the lamplighter

On `playing`, the four movement actions are read as held values, sampled once
per frame and applied to every tick that frame consumes. What the held values
do, the movement vector and `facing`, is in `specs/world.md`. Movement is the
only thing the player steers; every weapon fires on its own rhythm, as
`specs/weapons.md` states.

## The pointer

The pointer is read in the stage's own coordinates, `0` to `STAGE_W` across and
`0` to `STAGE_H` down, whatever the canvas's size on the page and wherever the
letterbox bars fall, and wheel travel is read in those same units.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The runtime layer you write maps both the event's client position and the
event's wheel travel through the same fit it draws under.
{{else}}
The {{engine.name}} engine delivers the position and the wheel travel already
in those units, and its documentation defines the API.
{{/if}}

Every screen that shows a vertical menu answers the pointer: `title`, `almanac`,
`levelup`, `paused`, `fallen`, and `dawn`. Each item the menu currently shows
occupies a rectangle on the stage, and no two of a screen's rectangles overlap.
On `title`, `levelup`, `paused`, `fallen`, and `dawn` that is every item of the
menu, and the rectangle at position `i` belongs to the item at `menuIndex` `i`.
On `almanac` it is the visible entry rows, at most `ALMANAC_ROWS` of them, and
the rectangle at position `i` belongs to the entry at `menuIndex`
`almanacScroll + i`. On `almanac` each tab of the tab bar occupies a rectangle
as well, disjoint from every entry rectangle.

Three rules, applied on every frame, after that frame's press edges and before
its update:

1. Hover. The pointer inside the rectangle of the item at `menuIndex` `i`, with
   `menuIndex` not `i`, sets `menuIndex` to `i` and plays `menu-move`. The
   pointer inside no rectangle changes nothing.
2. Click. A primary press edge inside the rectangle of the item at `menuIndex`
   `i` sets `menuIndex` to `i`, playing `menu-move` if that changed it, and then
   takes that item exactly as `confirm` on it does. A primary press edge inside
   no rectangle does nothing. On `almanac` an entry carries no `confirm`, so a
   click on an entry only moves the highlight; a click inside a tab's rectangle
   selects that tab exactly as `right` reaching it does.
3. The wheel. On `almanac`, wheel travel moves the list. A frame's travel is
   that frame's wheel deltas summed in stage units, divided by `WHEEL_ROW`
   (`100`) and truncated toward zero to give the number of rows `almanacScroll`
   moves, downward travel moving it toward the end of the list; the remainder is
   discarded, and `almanacScroll` is held within `0` and
   `max(0, count − ALMANAC_ROWS)`, `count` being the number of entries the shown
   tab holds. `menuIndex` is untouched by the wheel. On every other screen the
   wheel does nothing.

## What each screen reads

| Screen | Held | Press edges |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `title` | none | `up`, `down` move the highlight, wrapping; `confirm` takes the highlighted item; `mute` |
| `howto` | none | `back` returns to `title`; `mute` |
| `almanac` | none | `up`, `down` move the entry highlight, wrapping; `left`, `right` move the tab, wrapping; `back` returns to `title`; `mute` |
| `playing` | `up`, `down`, `left`, `right` | `pause` opens `paused`; `back` opens `paused`; `mute` |
| `levelup` | none | `up`, `down` move the highlight, wrapping; `confirm` accepts the highlighted offer; `mute` |
| `chest` | none | `confirm` closes the overlay; `mute` |
| `paused` | none | `up`, `down` move the highlight, wrapping; `confirm` takes the highlighted item; `pause` resumes; `back` resumes; `mute` |
| `fallen`, `dawn` | none | `up`, `down` move the highlight, wrapping; `confirm` takes the highlighted item; `back` returns to `title`; `mute` |

An action a row omits does nothing on that screen, so `confirm` on `almanac`
changes nothing, and `back` on `playing` does exactly what `pause` on `playing`
does. `menuIndex` is `0` on entering every screen, and on a screen with no
highlight it stays `0`. Where each `confirm` leads is in `specs/ui.md`.

## Out of scope

- Touch and gamepad input. The keyboard and the pointer are the whole of the
  game's input.
- Rebinding. The bindings are fixed and the game offers no way to change them.
